(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam-version-9"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Form E (70 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Albumin, the most abundant plasma protein, is important because it...","choices":["carries oxygen from the lungs to the peripheral tissues","acts as an antibody that neutralizes invading microbes","generates most of the plasma colloid osmotic pressure and transports many substances","forms the fibrin meshwork that seals a damaged vessel as it is usually described here"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Albumin is the chief contributor to plasma colloid (oncotic) osmotic pressure, keeping fluid within the vasculature, and it serves as a carrier for hormones, fatty acids, drugs, and other poorly soluble substances. Low albumin therefore causes edema. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-5"},{"q":"A reticulocyte count is useful clinically because reticulocytes are...","choices":["old red cells about to be removed by the spleen as it typically occurs here","a type of white cell that increases during infection","platelets that have just been released from megakaryocytes","immature red cells whose number reflects the marrow's production rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Reticulocytes are newly released, not-yet-mature red cells; a rising count signals that the marrow is actively producing red cells (for example, in response to blood loss), while a low count suggests impaired production. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Each hemoglobin molecule can carry up to four oxygen molecules because it contains...","choices":["four globin chains but only a single shared oxygen-binding pocket overall","four heme groups, each with an iron atom that binds one oxygen","a single large heme unit that binds all four oxygens at once","four copies of the iron-free protein globin without any heme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Hemoglobin is a tetramer with four polypeptide (globin) chains, each holding a heme group with a central iron (Fe2+) atom; each iron can reversibly bind one oxygen molecule, so one hemoglobin carries up to four. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 20-21"},{"q":"Erythropoietin secretion is governed by a negative feedback loop in which...","choices":["erythropoietin rises steadily regardless of oxygen levels under most normal conditions","low tissue oxygen raises erythropoietin, and restored oxygen delivery lowers it","the liver senses red cell count and sets erythropoietin directly","high tissue oxygen raises erythropoietin to make more red cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. When tissue oxygen delivery falls, the kidneys secrete more erythropoietin, stimulating red cell production; as the added red cells restore oxygen delivery, the stimulus subsides and erythropoietin falls, closing the loop and keeping red cell mass appropriate. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Iron absorbed from the diet is carried in the blood bound to...","choices":["albumin, the general carrier for all trace metals","bile salts, which shuttle it back to the liver in the body's tissues","transferrin, which delivers it to the marrow and storage sites","hemoglobin, which ferries it through the plasma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Absorbed iron is transported in plasma bound to transferrin, which delivers it to the bone marrow for hemoglobin synthesis and to the liver and other tissues for storage as ferritin; free iron is toxic, so it is kept protein-bound. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 17"},{"q":"The intrinsic and extrinsic pathways converge at a common pathway that begins with the activation of...","choices":["plasminogen, which initiates clot formation","tissue factor, which is unique to the intrinsic pathway","factor X, leading to prothrombin activator formation","fibrinogen, which is the first factor of the cascade"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both initiating pathways lead to activation of factor X; activated factor X (with factor V, calcium, and phospholipid) forms prothrombin activator, which converts prothrombin to thrombin. This shared final sequence is the common pathway. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 44-47"},{"q":"After a clot forms, clot retraction occurs as platelets...","choices":["contract and pull the fibrin strands together, drawing wound edges closer","dissolve the fibrin to reopen the vessel immediately","convert fibrin back into soluble fibrinogen during this particular process","release erythropoietin to replace lost red cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Platelets trapped in the clot contract, pulling on the fibrin meshwork; this retracts the clot, squeezes out serum, and draws the edges of the damaged vessel closer together, aiding healing. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-43"},{"q":"Fibrinolysis is triggered when plasminogen activators such as tPA...","choices":["cross-link fibrin strands to strengthen the clot","convert plasmin back into inactive plasminogen","convert plasminogen trapped in the clot into active plasmin","stimulate platelets to build a larger clot during this process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) and similar activators convert clot-bound plasminogen into plasmin, the enzyme that digests fibrin; this gradually dissolves the clot once the vessel has healed and restores normal flow. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 40-48"},{"q":"A very low platelet count tends to cause bleeding because platelets are needed to...","choices":["dissolve clots so blood can keep flowing freely","carry the oxygen required for wound healing during this particular process","produce the antibodies that fight wound infection","form the initial plug and support the reactions of the clotting cascade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Platelets form the primary hemostatic plug at sites of injury and provide the phospholipid surface on which clotting reactions assemble; when their number is too low, both plug formation and clot generation are impaired, producing a bleeding tendency. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 30-34"},{"q":"A rise in the circulating white blood cell count (leukocytosis) commonly indicates...","choices":["an active infection or inflammatory process in the body","a fall in the number of red blood cells present as a rule","an excess of platelets circulating in the blood","a reduced ability of the body to fight infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytosis, an increase in circulating white cells, is a typical response to infection or inflammation, as the marrow releases and produces more leukocytes to meet the demand; the pattern of which cells rise can hint at the cause. — Blood & Immunology, slides 26-30"},{"q":"Macrophages play a dual role in immunity because they both...","choices":["phagocytize pathogens and present their antigens to activate lymphocytes","secrete acid and also cross-link fibrin strands during this particular process","form blood clots and also filter lymph in the nodes","produce antibodies and also carry oxygen to tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages are powerful phagocytes that engulf and destroy pathogens, and they also display fragments of the ingested antigen on MHC to helper T-cells, bridging the innate and adaptive responses — a key dual function. — Blood & Immunology, slides 29-31, 55"},{"q":"Because lymphatics return interstitial fluid to the blood, blockage of lymphatic drainage typically causes...","choices":["an immediate rise in the arterial blood pressure","a rapid fall in the volume of interstitial fluid","accumulation of fluid in the tissues, producing edema","complete loss of the body's ability to make antibodies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lymphatic system continuously returns filtered interstitial fluid (and protein) to the bloodstream; if drainage is blocked, that fluid accumulates in the tissue, causing swelling (edema). This links lymphatic function to fluid balance as well as immunity. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22-24"},{"q":"Primary lymphoid organs differ from secondary lymphoid organs in that primary organs are where lymphocytes...","choices":["are formed and mature, whereas secondary organs are where they meet antigen","encounter antigen, whereas secondary organs form them","produce antibodies, whereas secondary organs make red cells","are destroyed, whereas secondary organs store them as it is usually described here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Primary lymphoid organs (bone marrow and thymus) are where lymphocytes originate and mature; secondary lymphoid organs (lymph nodes, spleen, tonsils) are where mature lymphocytes encounter antigen and mount responses. — Blood & Immunology, slides 22-24, 40-44"},{"q":"When activated by their specific antigen, B-lymphocytes proliferate and differentiate into...","choices":["plasma cells that secrete large amounts of antibody","red blood cells that transport the antibody in plasma","cytotoxic cells that directly lyse infected host cells","phagocytes that engulf and digest the antigen"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antigen-activated B-cells (with helper T-cell support) proliferate and differentiate into plasma cells, which are antibody factories secreting large quantities of the specific antibody, while some become memory B-cells for future responses. — Blood & Immunology, slides 44-50"},{"q":"The complement system aids defense in several ways, including...","choices":["producing the specific antibodies against a pathogen","opsonizing microbes, attracting phagocytes, and lysing target cells","storing iron released from broken-down red cells","carrying oxygen to the site of an infection under most conditions in the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Complement enhances immunity in multiple ways: it opsonizes microbes to promote phagocytosis, its fragments attract phagocytes (chemotaxis) and drive inflammation, and its terminal components form a membrane attack complex that lyses target cells. — Blood & Immunology, slides 52-53"},{"q":"Humoral immunity and cell-mediated immunity differ chiefly in that humoral immunity...","choices":["uses T-cells directly, while cell-mediated immunity uses antibodies","provides only immediate barriers such as skin and mucus during this particular process in the tissues","works through antibodies in body fluids, while cell-mediated immunity uses T-cells directly","is part of the innate system, while cell-mediated is acquired"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Humoral (antibody-mediated) immunity relies on antibodies secreted by plasma cells that circulate in body fluids to target extracellular threats, whereas cell-mediated immunity uses T-cells (especially cytotoxic T-cells) to act against intracellular pathogens and abnormal cells. — Blood & Immunology, slides 44-59"},{"q":"Natural killer (NK) cells are considered part of innate immunity because they...","choices":["can only act after memory cells have formed during this process in the tissues","mature in the thymus and require MHC-matched antigen","produce highly specific antibodies after being primed","destroy virus-infected and abnormal cells without needing prior sensitization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. NK cells recognize and kill virus-infected and tumor cells based on general 'danger' signals and altered surface markers, acting quickly without the antigen-specific priming that adaptive lymphocytes require — which places them in the innate arm. — Blood & Immunology, slides 33, 59-60"},{"q":"In the sequence of an acute inflammatory response, which cells typically arrive first, followed by which?","choices":["macrophages arrive first, followed by neutrophils days later","neutrophils arrive first, followed later by monocytes that become macrophages","red blood cells arrive first to wall off the infection","lymphocytes arrive first, before any phagocytes appear under most normal conditions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are the earliest cellular responders, arriving within about an hour; over the following hours to days, monocytes migrate in and mature into macrophages that continue phagocytosis and clean-up, giving the characteristic two-wave cellular response. — Blood & Immunology, slides 26-31"},{"q":"During swallowing, the lower esophageal sphincter must relax to...","choices":["push stomach acid up into the mouth for buffering","allow the bolus to pass from the esophagus into the stomach","release bile into the esophagus to begin digestion","prevent any food from entering the stomach at all during this process"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As a peristaltic wave carries the bolus down the esophagus, the lower esophageal sphincter relaxes to let it enter the stomach, then closes again to help prevent reflux of acidic gastric contents back into the esophagus. — GI Physiology, slides 40-45"},{"q":"When acidic, fatty chyme enters the duodenum, hormonal signals act to...","choices":["slow gastric emptying so the small intestine is not overwhelmed","stop all pancreatic secretion until the stomach empties in most cases","accelerate gastric emptying to clear the stomach quickly","trigger immediate defecation of the intestinal contents"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fat and acid in the duodenum trigger enterogastric signals (including CCK and secretin) that slow gastric emptying; this ensures the duodenum has time to neutralize acid and digest fat before receiving more chyme. — GI Physiology, slides 13-15, 46"},{"q":"Within the gastric glands, the division of labor is such that...","choices":["chief cells secrete bile and parietal cells secrete enzymes","both cell types secrete only mucus to protect the lining during this particular process","parietal cells secrete pepsinogen while chief cells secrete acid","chief cells secrete pepsinogen while parietal cells secrete acid and intrinsic factor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chief cells release pepsinogen (activated to pepsin by acid), while parietal cells secrete hydrochloric acid (which also activates pepsinogen and kills microbes) and intrinsic factor needed for vitamin B12 absorption. Each cell type has a distinct secretory role. — GI Physiology, slides 82-90"},{"q":"Bile contains both bile salts and bile pigments; the bile pigments (mainly bilirubin) are...","choices":["waste products of hemoglobin breakdown excreted in the bile","hormones that stimulate pancreatic secretion during this process","enzymes that digest carbohydrates in the duodenum","the active agents that emulsify fat in the intestine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Bile pigments such as bilirubin are waste from the breakdown of heme (from old red cells); the liver conjugates and excretes them in bile. In contrast, bile salts are the functional emulsifiers derived from cholesterol. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 13-17"},{"q":"Secreting pancreatic proteases as inactive zymogens is important because it...","choices":["makes the enzymes work better in the acidic stomach","lets the pancreas store the enzymes for years at a time","allows the enzymes to digest carbohydrates instead of protein","prevents the enzymes from digesting the pancreas that makes them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. If active proteases were produced within the pancreas, they would digest the organ's own tissue; secreting them as inactive zymogens (activated only in the intestine) protects the pancreas from self-digestion, a safeguard whose failure underlies pancreatitis. — GI Physiology, slides 101-105"},{"q":"During fasting, the liver helps maintain blood glucose by gluconeogenesis, which is the...","choices":["absorption of glucose directly from the digested food","conversion of glucose into fat for long-term storage","synthesis of new glucose from noncarbohydrate sources such as amino acids and lactate","breakdown of stored glycogen into glucose molecules during this process in the tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Gluconeogenesis is the liver's production of glucose from noncarbohydrate precursors — amino acids (from protein), lactate, and glycerol — sustaining blood glucose once glycogen stores are exhausted during fasting, which is vital for glucose-dependent tissues like the brain. — Ch 71 Liver, slide 9; Ch 68, slides 25-27"},{"q":"The small intestine's absorptive surface is vastly increased by...","choices":["a single large chamber that holds food for a long time","circular folds, villi, and microvilli that multiply the surface area","thick muscular walls that grind the food finely under most normal conditions","a smooth, flat lining that speeds the passage of food"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three levels of folding — the circular folds of the wall, the finger-like villi, and the microvilli of the brush border — together increase the absorptive surface area enormously, maximizing contact between nutrients and the absorptive cells. — GI Physiology, slides 120-122, 140"},{"q":"Much of the absorption of nutrients like glucose and amino acids is coupled to...","choices":["the digestion of water into hydrogen and oxygen","the breakdown of the nutrients by stomach acid in most cases","the transport of sodium, whose gradient powers their uptake","the emulsifying action of bile salts on the nutrients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Glucose and amino acids are absorbed by sodium-coupled cotransporters: the inward sodium gradient (maintained by the basolateral Na+/K+ ATPase) drives these nutrients into the enterocyte against their own gradients, an example of secondary active transport. — GI Physiology, slides 126-129"},{"q":"The colon's pouch-like haustra are formed and their contractions serve to...","choices":["absorb the bulk of dietary fat and protein","mix and slowly move contents while water is absorbed","rapidly propel contents straight to the rectum","secrete digestive enzymes to finish breaking down food"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The haustra are sacculations produced by the colon's muscle arrangement; their slow, segmenting contractions (haustration) knead the contents and expose them to the mucosa for water and electrolyte absorption, gradually moving material along toward the rectum. — GI Physiology, slides 59-66"},{"q":"The liver's contribution to fat digestion is its production of...","choices":["bile, which emulsifies fat to aid its digestion and absorption","hydrochloric acid, which breaks fat into fatty acids","insulin, which drives fat storage after a meal as a general rule","lipase, the main enzyme that digests dietary fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The liver continuously produces bile, whose bile salts emulsify dietary fat into small droplets; this greatly increases the surface area for pancreatic lipase to act and helps form the micelles that carry fat products to the absorptive surface. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 13-17"},{"q":"Glucagon raises blood glucose during fasting mainly by stimulating the liver to...","choices":["convert blood glucose directly into body protein","break down glycogen and produce new glucose for release","stop producing glucose so that levels can fall","take up glucose from the blood and store it as glycogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon, secreted when blood glucose is low, signals the liver to carry out glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis and to release glucose into the blood, counteracting hypoglycemia — the opposite of insulin's storage-promoting actions. — Ch 68, slide 30; Ch 71, slide 9"},{"q":"During a sudden fight-or-flight response, epinephrine helps supply energy by...","choices":["converting glucose into structural protein in the body's tissues","blocking the release of glucose into the blood","promoting glycogen storage to conserve glucose","stimulating rapid glycogen breakdown to raise blood glucose"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Epinephrine promotes glycogenolysis in the liver (and muscle), quickly liberating glucose to fuel a stress response; it also mobilizes fatty acids, providing a rapid surge of available energy substrates. — Ch 68, slide 30; Ch 71, slide 9"},{"q":"The brain relies heavily on a steady glucose supply because it...","choices":["produces its own glucose independently of the blood","stores large amounts of glycogen for its own use during this process in the tissues","normally uses glucose as its main fuel and stores very little of its own","can only use fatty acids delivered from adipose tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Under normal conditions the brain depends on a continuous supply of blood glucose as its primary fuel and holds almost no energy reserve of its own, which is why hypoglycemia rapidly impairs brain function (though ketones can partly substitute during prolonged fasting). — Ch 68, slides 25-27; Ch 73, slide 12"},{"q":"When carbohydrate intake exceeds what can be stored as glycogen, the excess is...","choices":["excreted unchanged by the kidneys in the urine","stored indefinitely as additional glycogen without limit","turned directly into muscle protein for growth","converted to fat (lipogenesis) and stored in adipose tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once the limited glycogen stores are filled, surplus carbohydrate is converted through lipogenesis into fatty acids and triglyceride, which are stored in adipose tissue; this is a major route by which excess dietary carbohydrate contributes to body fat. — Ch 69, slides 20-23; Ch 68, slide 24"},{"q":"Certain fatty acids are called 'essential' because they...","choices":["cannot be synthesized by the body and must be obtained from the diet","can be produced from glucose whenever they are needed","are the only fatty acids the body can store as fat during this process","provide far more energy per gram than any other fat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Essential fatty acids (such as linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid) cannot be made by the body and must come from food; they are needed for cell membranes and as precursors of signaling molecules, so their dietary intake is required. — Ch 69, slides 3-5; Ch 72, slides 20-22"},{"q":"Fat is the body's most efficient long-term energy store largely because it...","choices":["provides the least energy per gram of any fuel under most normal conditions","can be used only by the liver and not by other tissues","is stored with large amounts of water for quick access","yields more energy per gram and is stored with little associated water"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fat provides about 9 kcal per gram (versus ~4 for carbohydrate or protein) and is stored in a nearly anhydrous form, so it packs far more energy per unit weight than glycogen, which is stored with substantial water — making fat the ideal compact reserve. — Ch 72, slides 4-5; Ch 73, slide 12"},{"q":"The body maintains an amino-acid pool that is best described as...","choices":["a reserve used only for energy and never for building protein","a set of amino acids that cannot be exchanged or replaced","a fixed, permanent store of protein that is never used under most conditions in the body","a dynamic supply drawn on for protein synthesis and replenished by turnover and diet"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Free amino acids form a pool that is continuously used to build new proteins and replenished by dietary intake and by the breakdown (turnover) of existing body proteins; this balance lets the body remodel and repair proteins constantly. — Ch 70, slides 3-6"},{"q":"The urea cycle is the body's solution to the problem that...","choices":["amino acids contain too little nitrogen to be useful","the body cannot break down amino acids at all during this particular process in the tissues","ammonia from amino-acid breakdown is toxic and must be converted for safe excretion","glucose must be converted into protein for storage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Deamination of amino acids releases ammonia, which is toxic even at low levels; the liver's urea cycle converts ammonia into urea, a far safer compound that the kidneys excrete, allowing the body to dispose of nitrogen without ammonia toxicity. — Ch 70, slides 16-17"},{"q":"Growth hormone promotes a positive nitrogen balance by...","choices":["converting body protein directly into stored fat","accelerating the breakdown of tissue protein for fuel","stimulating amino-acid uptake and protein synthesis in tissues","blocking all amino acids from entering the cells in the body's tissues"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Growth hormone enhances the transport of amino acids into cells and stimulates protein synthesis while sparing protein from breakdown; the net anabolic effect builds tissue protein, producing a positive nitrogen balance during growth. — Ch 70, slide 19"},{"q":"Ghrelin, secreted largely by the stomach, influences feeding by...","choices":["digesting fats directly within the small intestine","suppressing hunger over the long term as fat stores grow","stimulating hunger and rising before meals to prompt eating","ending each meal by signaling fullness to the brain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ghrelin is often called the 'hunger hormone': its levels rise before meals and fall after eating, and it acts on the hypothalamus to stimulate appetite, in contrast to satiety signals like leptin and CCK that reduce intake. — Ch 72, slides 11-14"},{"q":"The glucostatic idea of appetite regulation proposes that...","choices":["a rise in blood glucose after a meal increases hunger","a fall in blood glucose availability increases hunger and prompts eating","blood glucose has no relationship to appetite at all under these conditions","only long-term fat stores, not glucose, affect appetite"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The glucostatic theory holds that hypothalamic centers monitor glucose availability: when the level or utilization of glucose falls, hunger increases and feeding is stimulated; when it rises after a meal, satiety centers are activated, helping regulate short-term intake. — Ch 72, slides 9-12"},{"q":"During prolonged starvation, ketone bodies become important because they...","choices":["are stored in large amounts before starvation begins","provide an alternative fuel for the brain, sparing body protein","prevent the body from using any fat for energy in the body's tissues","are the only fuel the body can produce from glucose"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As fasting continues, the brain adapts to use ketone bodies produced by the liver from fat; this reduces the brain's demand for glucose and therefore lessens the breakdown of body protein for gluconeogenesis, prolonging survival. — Ch 72, slides 17-19; Ch 69, slides 15-19"},{"q":"A stable body temperature is maintained when...","choices":["heat produced by metabolism is balanced by heat lost to the environment","heat loss always greatly exceeds heat production","the body stops producing any heat from metabolism","heat production always greatly exceeds heat loss under most normal conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Body temperature stays constant when heat gain (chiefly from metabolism, plus any absorbed from the environment) equals heat loss (by radiation, conduction, convection, and evaporation); the hypothalamus adjusts these processes to keep the balance. — Ch 74, slides 2-6"},{"q":"Sweating is unusual among sympathetic responses because the sweat glands are activated by...","choices":["cholinergic sympathetic fibers, using acetylcholine rather than norepinephrine","local reflexes independent of any nervous input during this process in the tissues","parasympathetic fibers that override the sympathetic system","circulating thyroid hormone acting directly on the glands"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Although sweat glands are innervated by the sympathetic nervous system, their postganglionic fibers are cholinergic, releasing acetylcholine; this is an exception to the usual sympathetic transmitter norepinephrine, and it drives thermoregulatory sweating. — Ch 74, slides 8-14"},{"q":"Countercurrent heat exchange in the limbs helps conserve heat by...","choices":["evaporating water from the surface of the deep vessels","transferring heat from outgoing arterial blood to returning venous blood","preventing any blood from returning to the core as it is usually described here","carrying all the warm blood to the skin surface to radiate heat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In the limbs, deep veins run alongside arteries so that warm arterial blood heading outward gives up heat to cooler venous blood returning to the core; this countercurrent exchange retains heat centrally and reduces loss from the extremities in the cold. — Ch 74, slides 11-14"},{"q":"Basal metabolic rate and resting metabolic rate differ in that basal metabolic rate is measured under...","choices":["stricter standardized conditions of fasting and complete rest","a state immediately following a large meal","exposure to cold that triggers shivering","conditions of heavy physical exertion under most conditions in the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Basal metabolic rate is measured under rigorous conditions — after an overnight fast, fully rested, and at a comfortable temperature — whereas resting metabolic rate is measured under less strict resting conditions and is usually slightly higher; both estimate baseline energy needs. — Ch 73, slides 8-10"},{"q":"Physical activity raises total energy expenditure primarily because...","choices":["contracting muscles greatly increase their consumption of energy substrates","the basal metabolic rate itself falls during exercise","digestion of food stops so all energy goes to movement","body temperature drops, forcing more heat production during this process in the tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Exercise is the most variable component of energy expenditure: working muscles sharply increase their use of glucose and fatty acids to fuel contraction, raising oxygen consumption and total metabolic rate well above the resting level. — Ch 73, slides 10-12"},{"q":"Many vitamins are required in the diet because they function as...","choices":["major sources of energy, like carbohydrates and fats","structural building blocks of muscle and bone tissue","coenzymes or their precursors, assisting enzymes in metabolic reactions","hormones produced by the body's endocrine glands as it typically occurs here"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Numerous vitamins (especially the B-complex) serve as coenzymes or coenzyme precursors that enzymes need to catalyze metabolic reactions; because the body cannot synthesize them in adequate amounts, they must be supplied by the diet, and deficiency impairs the linked pathways. — Ch 72, slides 20-24"},{"q":"Minerals such as calcium and iron are essential nutrients because, respectively, they...","choices":["act as the main digestive enzymes in the gut during this process in the tissues","support bone structure and nerve/muscle function, and form part of hemoglobin","provide most of the body's daily energy needs","are stored as fat for long-term energy reserves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Calcium is needed for bone and teeth and for nerve conduction and muscle contraction, while iron is a key component of hemoglobin (and other proteins) for oxygen transport; both are required in the diet for these specific roles. — Ch 72, slides 24-26"},{"q":"External respiration and internal respiration refer, respectively, to gas exchange...","choices":["between blood and tissues, and between alveoli and blood as a general rule","only within the airways, and only within the bloodstream","between the nose and the trachea, and within the alveoli","between alveoli and blood, and between blood and the body's tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. External respiration is the exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide between the alveoli and the pulmonary blood, while internal respiration is the exchange between the systemic blood and the metabolizing tissues; both rely on diffusion down partial-pressure gradients. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 3-6, 71-74"},{"q":"Emphysema and pulmonary fibrosis affect compliance in opposite ways: emphysema tends to...","choices":["stiffen the lung, while fibrosis makes it easier to inflate","make the lung easier to inflate and to recoil normally","increase compliance by destroying elastic tissue, while fibrosis stiffens the lung","have no measurable effect on lung compliance at all as it is usually described here"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Emphysema destroys alveolar walls and elastic fibers, so the lung becomes floppy and easier to inflate (higher compliance) but recoils poorly; fibrosis lays down stiff tissue, lowering compliance so the lung is harder to inflate. They alter lung mechanics in opposite directions. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 19-21"},{"q":"Minute ventilation is calculated as...","choices":["the inspiratory reserve minus the expiratory reserve","the residual volume divided by the tidal volume","tidal volume multiplied by the respiratory rate","vital capacity multiplied by the residual volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Minute ventilation is the total volume of air moved per minute, equal to the tidal volume of each breath times the number of breaths per minute; it represents total airflow, though not all of it reaches the alveoli for exchange. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"Alveolar ventilation is a better measure of effective breathing than minute ventilation because it...","choices":["measures only the residual volume left after expiration","ignores the tidal volume entirely in its calculation as a general rule","counts all air moved, including that filling the airways","accounts for dead space, reflecting only air that reaches the alveoli"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Alveolar ventilation subtracts the dead-space volume (air in conducting airways that never reaches alveoli) from each breath, so it reflects only the air available for gas exchange; this is why slow, deep breathing can be more effective than rapid, shallow breathing at the same minute ventilation. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"Insufficient surfactant, as in a premature newborn, leads to breathing difficulty because...","choices":["the alveoli become too easy to inflate and overexpand","alveoli tend to collapse, so much greater effort is needed to reinflate them","gas exchange speeds up beyond what the blood can handle","surface tension in the alveoli drops too low under most conditions throughout the body"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Without adequate surfactant, alveolar surface tension is high; alveoli collapse at the end of expiration, and reopening them requires large pressures, greatly increasing the work of breathing — the basis of neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-24"},{"q":"In the upright lung, ventilation and perfusion are not perfectly matched; at the apex compared with the base there is...","choices":["relatively more ventilation than perfusion (a higher V/Q ratio)","exactly equal ventilation and perfusion throughout as a general rule","relatively more perfusion than ventilation than the base","no ventilation and no perfusion reaching that region"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gravity makes both ventilation and perfusion greater at the base, but perfusion increases more steeply, so the apex has a relatively higher ventilation-to-perfusion ratio, while the base has a relatively lower one. This regional variation is normal in the upright lung. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 64-66"},{"q":"A ventilation-perfusion mismatch can take two forms: a shunt is best described as...","choices":["complete absence of both airflow and blood flow","blood passing alveoli that are perfused but not ventilated","air reaching alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused","equal ventilation and perfusion in every alveolus"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A shunt is perfusion without adequate ventilation — blood flows past alveoli that receive little or no air, so it is not oxygenated. Its counterpart, dead space, is ventilation without perfusion (alveoli receive air but no blood flow). — Pulmonary I & II, slides 64-66"},{"q":"In a healthy lung, oxygen uptake is described as perfusion-limited, meaning that...","choices":["the membrane is too thick for oxygen to cross at all","blood becomes fully oxygenated so quickly that only increasing blood flow raises uptake","oxygen can never fully equilibrate with the blood during this particular process in the tissues","blood flow has no effect on how much oxygen is taken up"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Normally, oxygen equilibrates between alveolus and blood long before the blood leaves the capillary, so the amount taken up is limited by how much blood flows through (perfusion), not by diffusion; only in disease with a thickened membrane does diffusion become the limiting factor. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 67-69"},{"q":"During heavy exercise, forced expiration becomes active and is driven largely by...","choices":["contraction of the abdominal and internal intercostal muscles","relaxation of the diaphragm alone, as in quiet breathing","the release of surfactant that forces air out under these conditions","contraction of the diaphragm to push air outward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Whereas quiet expiration is passive, forced expiration recruits the abdominal muscles (which push the diaphragm upward) and the internal intercostals (which pull the ribs down), actively decreasing thoracic volume to expel air rapidly during exertion. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-14"},{"q":"The respiratory membrane is well suited to gas exchange because it is...","choices":["lined with cartilage that supports rapid exchange in the body's tissues","extremely thin, so gases have only a very short distance to diffuse","quite thick, which slows diffusion but filters the air","covered by thick mucus that traps the diffusing gases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The respiratory membrane is only about 0.2 to 0.6 micrometers thick, giving oxygen and carbon dioxide a very short diffusion path; combined with its enormous surface area, this thinness makes gas exchange rapid and efficient. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 57-60"},{"q":"The pontine (pneumotaxic) center influences breathing mainly by...","choices":["generating the fundamental breathing rhythm on its own","contracting the diaphragm directly through the phrenic nerve","sensing arterial oxygen at the carotid bodies under most conditions in the body","limiting the duration of inspiration and helping set the breathing rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The pneumotaxic center in the pons sends signals that switch off inspiration, thereby limiting inspiratory time; a stronger signal shortens inspiration and quickens the rate, so this center helps fine-tune the rhythm generated by the medulla. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 88-90"},{"q":"The reason hemoglobin is essential for oxygen transport is that, without it,...","choices":["the small amount of oxygen dissolved in plasma could not meet tissue demands","carbon dioxide would be unable to leave the tissues","the lungs would be unable to ventilate at all during this process in the tissues","oxygen could not diffuse across the respiratory membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oxygen is poorly soluble, so plasma alone carries only a tiny fraction of the body's needs; hemoglobin increases the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity many-fold by binding oxygen, which is why anemia (low hemoglobin) so seriously reduces oxygen delivery. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 76-77"},{"q":"A rightward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve is caused by conditions such as...","choices":["a complete absence of carbon dioxide in the tissues","the presence of carbon monoxide in the blood during this process","decreased CO2, increased pH, and lower temperature","increased CO2, increased acidity, and higher temperature"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Higher CO2, lower pH (more acid), higher temperature, and increased 2,3-BPG all shift the curve rightward, lowering hemoglobin's oxygen affinity and promoting oxygen release; these are exactly the conditions found in active, metabolizing tissue that needs more oxygen. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 80-81"},{"q":"A leftward shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve, indicating higher affinity, occurs with...","choices":["decreased temperature, decreased CO2, and the presence of fetal hemoglobin","increased temperature and increased acidity in the tissues","a rise in CO2 and hydrogen ion concentration as it is usually described here","heavy exercise that raises the tissue temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lower temperature, lower CO2/acidity, decreased 2,3-BPG, fetal hemoglobin, and carbon monoxide all shift the curve leftward, raising oxygen affinity so hemoglobin binds oxygen more tightly and releases it less readily at the tissues. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 80-82"},{"q":"The chloride shift accompanies bicarbonate handling because, as bicarbonate leaves the red cell,...","choices":["oxygen is forced out of the cell in exchange in most cases","chloride enters the cell to keep it electrically balanced","the cell manufactures new hemoglobin to compensate","sodium leaves the cell to replace the lost charge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Carbonic anhydrase generates bicarbonate inside the red cell; as bicarbonate diffuses out into plasma, chloride moves in (the chloride, or Hamburger, shift) to preserve electrical neutrality, allowing large amounts of CO2 to be carried as plasma bicarbonate. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-87"},{"q":"Compared with the oxygen curve, the carbon dioxide dissociation relationship is steeper and more linear, which means blood can...","choices":["exchange CO2 only in the tissues and never in the lungs","load and unload large amounts of CO2 with modest changes in its partial pressure","only transport CO2 that is bound directly to oxygen during this process in the tissues","carry no carbon dioxide unless its pressure is extremely high"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because the CO2 dissociation curve is steeper and nearly linear over the physiologic range, relatively small changes in PCO2 produce large changes in CO2 content, so the blood can take up CO2 at the tissues and release it at the lungs efficiently with modest pressure differences. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-87"},{"q":"Acidosis and alkalosis are defined by blood pH such that acidosis is a...","choices":["shift toward a higher-than-normal pH (fewer hydrogen ions)","condition affecting only oxygen levels, not pH","shift toward a lower-than-normal pH (more hydrogen ions)","state in which blood pH cannot change at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acidosis refers to a process lowering blood pH below the normal range (an increase in hydrogen ion concentration), while alkalosis refers to a process raising pH above normal; each can arise from respiratory (CO2) or metabolic causes. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"A disturbance is called a respiratory (rather than metabolic) acid-base problem when it originates from...","choices":["the loss of stomach acid through vomiting","an accumulation of lactic acid during exercise","a change in CO2 caused by altered ventilation","a change in bicarbonate produced by the kidneys"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Respiratory acid-base disturbances arise from changes in CO2 due to hypo- or hyperventilation (CO2 retention lowers pH; excess CO2 removal raises it), whereas metabolic disturbances arise from changes in bicarbonate or fixed acids independent of CO2. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"At the tissues, carbon dioxide moves from the cells into the blood because...","choices":["the blood actively pumps carbon dioxide out of the cells","the PCO2 of metabolizing cells is higher than that of the arriving blood","cell PCO2 is always lower than that of the blood under most conditions in the body","oxygen forces the carbon dioxide out of the cells"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Metabolizing cells continuously produce CO2, keeping their PCO2 above that of the arterial blood entering the capillaries; CO2 therefore diffuses down its partial-pressure gradient from the cells into the blood to be carried to the lungs. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 71-74"},{"q":"Peripheral chemoreceptors also respond to blood pH, so a fall in pH (acidosis) tends to...","choices":["suppress ventilation, allowing CO2 to accumulate further","cause the kidneys to immediately exhale the excess acid","have no effect on breathing, since only CO2 matters during this process","stimulate ventilation, helping to remove CO2 and correct the pH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The carotid and aortic bodies sense a fall in pH and increase their signaling to the respiratory center; the resulting rise in ventilation blows off CO2, which lowers carbonic acid and helps raise the pH back toward normal — a respiratory compensation for acidosis. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 95-99"},{"q":"Formation of the platelet plug follows the sequence of...","choices":["dissolution of the vessel wall to release clotting factors","aggregation first, then adhesion, and finally activation under these conditions","conversion of fibrinogen to fibrin before any platelets act","adhesion to the injured wall, activation, then aggregation into a plug"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Platelets first adhere to exposed collagen at the injury (aided by von Willebrand factor), then become activated and release mediators such as ADP and thromboxane A2, which recruit and cause more platelets to aggregate, building the primary hemostatic plug. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 30-34"},{"q":"Interferons contribute to innate defense against viruses by...","choices":["signaling neighboring cells to resist viral replication","carrying oxygen to virus-infected tissues","forming the membrane attack complex that lyses cells","directly binding and neutralizing circulating antibodies"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Virus-infected cells release interferons, which act on nearby cells to induce an antiviral state — producing proteins that interfere with viral replication — thereby limiting the spread of the virus before the adaptive response is fully mobilized. — Blood & Immunology, slides 52-53, 60"},{"q":"The stomach lining is protected from its own acid and pepsin mainly by...","choices":["a continuous flow of bile that neutralizes the acid in the body","rapid peristalsis that keeps acid away from the lining","a layer of mucus and bicarbonate secreted over the epithelium","the complete absence of acid in contact with the wall"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surface mucous cells secrete a thick, bicarbonate-rich mucus layer that coats the gastric epithelium; this barrier neutralizes acid at the cell surface and shields the lining from pepsin, so the stomach can hold strong acid without digesting itself. — GI Physiology, slides 82-90"}]};
